Vendored for Unsloth Studio's managed Kaggle TPU connection so end users do
not need to clone or install the launcher separately.

Local Studio changes (mlx.23): invoke the same-interpreter Kaggle CLI; private
per-connection state and kernel names; --json progress; official Kaggle log
fallback; validated reconnect; bounded subprocesses and propagated stop errors.
The TPU serving kernel and embedded MTP patch remain upstream's implementation.
studio_bridge.py is a local MIT-licensed launcher adapter, covered by LICENSE.

Local Studio changes (mlx.24): explicit TpuV5E8 allocation; a separate
studio_preflight.py is embedded by the launcher around guarded source markers
to reject CPU-only VMs before installation and verify eight JAX TPU devices
before vLLM starts. The upstream kernel file and its MTP patch are unchanged.
One automatic retry is allowed only after a retryable preflight failure and
confirmed terminal session. Run markers reject previous-version Kaggle logs.
Failure diagnosis preserves the actual step/root cause and excludes harmless
GCE metadata warnings; compile progress reports elapsed minutes.

Local Studio changes (mlx.25): status/log read timeouts no longer terminate
the watcher; transient status failures retry up to five reads before detaching
without stopping/restarting the notebook. Queue updates show elapsed minutes
and distinguish allocation from model installation. Terminal Kaggle errors
before boot are identified separately. Notification payloads are validated.

Local Studio changes (mlx.26): Studio no longer requests its TPU by committing
the serving notebook with `kernels push`. A small private CPU-only notebook is
created once to own the dataset mounts, then Kaggle's public create-session API
opens an interactive session and the unchanged prepared upstream kernel runs
through its authenticated Jupyter websocket. The interactive API's documented
machine-shape identifier is `Tpu1VmV38`; the in-VM preflight remains the source
of truth and requires eight real TPU devices. Stop cancels kernelSessionId and
preserves the private bootstrap for the next Start. Jupyter URLs/tokens are
never written to Studio state. The non-Studio CLI keeps the upstream batch path.

Local Studio changes (mlx.27): interactive TPU allocation no longer has a
Studio-side wall-clock timeout. Kaggle can keep a CreateKernelSession request
pending for longer than 15 minutes even with a valid account/token; Studio now
keeps reporting PROVISIONING on that same operation until Kaggle completes it or
the user presses Stop. Explicit finite timeouts remain available to tests and
callers that request one. No extra TPU session is submitted automatically.

Local Studio changes (mlx.28): while CreateKernelSession remains pending,
Studio also queries Kaggle's kernel-session worker status. QUEUED now reports
elapsed queue minutes (and TPU quota remaining when the quota endpoint provides
it); RUNNING reports that hardware was assigned and Jupyter is initializing.
Terminal worker errors/cancellation are surfaced instead of leaving a stale
allocation message. Reconnect uses the same read-only worker-state check and
never submits a duplicate session.
